Visual Snow Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Continuous flickering visual static, Persistence of symptoms with eyes closed, Prominent palinopsia (afterimages), Normal comprehensive ophthalmological examination

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe panic and extreme visual distress driven by sudden worsening of constant visual static and fear of impending blindness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing acute, severe visual disturbances (intense static, afterimages, and photophobia) leading to profound panic and absolute inability to function at home. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to perform a neurological and pupillary examination, definitively exclude an acute retinal detachment, acute optic neuritis, or an acute central neurological event, and safely manage the severe acute psychological crisis before daytime services open.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My entire vision is covered in a million tiny flickering dots, and when I look away from the TV, the image stays burned into my eyes. I'm going blind."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 24-hour severe acute exacerbation of a continuous, dynamic, and unremitting presence of countless tiny flickering dots scattered across the entire visual field. The patient describes this 'static' or 'snow-like' visual disruption as mimicking a poorly tuned analog television screen. The patient is profoundly distressed because the intense visual static persists perfectly equally even with the eyes firmly closed. The patient reports prominent palinopsia today, presenting as distinct afterimages and severe trailing of moving objects. The patient complains of exquisite, severe photophobia out of proportion to ambient lighting. Patient denies acute eye pain, unilateral visual field loss, or sudden total blackouts (amaurosis fugax).
Functional Status: Sitting in a completely darkened room with eyes closed, highly agitated, refusing to open eyes due to sensory overload.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound acute distress and panic; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, wearing sunglasses indoors, visibly trembling.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is globally intact. Visual acuity (assessed roughly with a reading card in dim light) appears grossly preserved centrally, without dense central scotomas. Confrontational visual fields are full and symmetrical, ruling out large retinal detachments or homonymous hemianopia. Extraocular movements are completely intact without pain. Pupillary direct and consensual reflexes are perfectly normal and symmetrical, ruling out a relative afferent pupillary defect (optic neuritis). Documented history of an entirely normal comprehensive ophthalmological examination (including a completely normal dilated fundoscopy) and completely normal structural neuroimaging 6 months ago for identical initial symptoms.
Environmental Observations: All light sources are aggressively blocked out; room is pitch black.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; avoids screens entirely.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Visual Snow Syndrome - Status: Acute perceptual hyper-arousal flare
Differentials:

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out explicitly by the full confrontational visual fields (no 'curtain' coming down), completely bilateral presentation, and lack of sudden large, dark, fixed floaters.

Optic Neuritis - Ruled out clinically by the strictly normal, symmetrical pupillary reflexes (no RAPD), complete lack of pain with eye movement, and bilateral presentation.

Migraine with Aura - Ruled out as the visual symptoms are completely continuous and unremitting for 24 hours, rather than a transient 20-60 minute evolving scintillating scotoma that definitively resolves.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a severe, acute sensory processing flare resulting in extreme visual distress and profound psychological panic regarding impending blindness. The intensity of the visual symptoms mandated an immediate, hands-on clinical neurological and gross ophthalmological examination. By definitively confirming intact pupillary reflexes, full visual fields, and normal gross acuity, the physician safely excluded sight-threatening acute structural emergencies like retinal detachment or optic neuritis. Providing immediate, concrete reassurance of intact visual hardware safely aborted the severe psychological crisis, preventing an unnecessary, high-stress emergency department transfer, strictly satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct, firm clinical reassurance regarding the completely normal pupillary responses and the intact structural nature of the eyes. Advised the patient to slowly reintroduce low-level ambient lighting to prevent dark-adaptation hypersensitivity.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of sitting in a pitch-black room with eyes squeezed shut, actively use FL-41 tinted lenses (or standard polarized sunglasses initially) in a softly lit room. Dark environments actually force the brain to turn up its visual 'gain', making the static significantly worse; introducing filtered, soft light gives the brain actual data to process, directly rewarding the patient with a measurable decrease in the intensity of the visual snow and reducing the severe photophobia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a neuro-ophthalmologist for specialized tinted lens prescription and reassurance.
DME/Safety (Optional): FL-41 tinted glasses.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to discuss central neuromodulators (e.g., Lamotrigine or Amitriptyline trial).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a sudden, solid black 'curtain' falling over one eye, sudden total loss of vision in one or both eyes, agonizing pain when moving the eyes, or sudden severe weakness in the face or limbs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the completely normal gross pupillary and field exam today; any development of a solid blind spot requires immediate ophthalmological review.
Clinical Course: Visual Snow is a neurological software issue where the brain's visual processing center is hyperactive, seeing the 'static' of its own nerve activity. Your eyes themselves are completely healthy. It will not cause you to go blind.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if a solid dark curtain falls over your vision, if you suddenly lose your sight completely, or if it becomes extremely painful to look around.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pupillary exams we just performed prove that your optic nerves and retinas are perfectly healthy and transmitting light normally. The static and afterimages occur because the visual center in your brain is 'turned up too loud' and cannot filter out normal neural noise. We must treat the brain's hyperactivity, not the eye.
Actionable Steps: Tomorrow, do not sit in complete darkness. Sit in a room with a soft, warm lamp turned on while wearing your sunglasses. Staying in the dark forces your brain to try even harder to see, which drastically amplifies the static. Providing a soft, continuous visual anchor will slowly force the brain to lower its internal volume.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Visual Snow Syndrome flare. Pupil/vision exam is totally normal; NO retinal detachment. Your eyes are physically safe. See your regular GP in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

Do not sit in a pitch-black room; use soft lighting and sunglasses to reduce static. Call 000 immediately if a black curtain falls over your vision or you lose sight.
